# Setup

## 1. Pull the Project from GitHub

Clone the repository:

```bash
git clone <YOUR_GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL>
cd <YOUR_PROJECT_DIRECTORY>
```

---

## 2. Build and Push the Docker Image

Go to the application directory:

```bash
cd application
```

Build the Docker image:

```bash
docker build -t <DOCKERHUB_USERNAME>/my-own-images:portfolio-app-v3 .
```

Login to Docker Hub:

```bash
docker login
```

Push the image:

```bash
docker push <DOCKERHUB_USERNAME>/my-own-images:portfolio-app-v3
```

The Docker image is now available in Docker Hub and will be used for deployment on the EC2 instances.

---

## 3. Create and Configure AWS Account

Create an AWS account.

Create an IAM user with the required programmatic access permissions.

Create an access key for the IAM user and keep the following credentials available:

```text
AWS Access Key ID
AWS Secret Access Key
```

Install the AWS CLI locally and configure it:

```bash
aws configure
```

Enter the required information:

```text
AWS Access Key ID:     <YOUR_ACCESS_KEY>
AWS Secret Access Key: <YOUR_SECRET_KEY>
Default region name:  ap-south-1
Default output format: json
```

Verify the AWS CLI configuration:

```bash
aws sts get-caller-identity
```

This confirms that the local machine can communicate with AWS.

---

# 4. Provision AWS Infrastructure Using Terraform

Move to the Terraform directory:

```bash
cd terraform
```

Initialize Terraform:

```bash
terraform init
```

Validate the Terraform configuration:

```bash
terraform validate
```

Review the infrastructure that Terraform will create:

```bash
terraform plan
```

Create the AWS infrastructure:

```bash
terraform apply
```

When prompted, enter:

```text
yes
```

Terraform will create two EC2 instances along with the required AWS resources such as the security group and key pair.

---

# 5. Get the EC2 IP Addresses

After Terraform finishes, get the outputs:

```bash
terraform output
```

Example:

```text
ec2_public_ips = [
  "<EC2_IP_1>",
  "<EC2_IP_2>"
]
```

The two public IP addresses will be used in the Ansible inventory.

---

# 6. Configure Ansible

Move to the Ansible directory:

```bash
cd ../ansible
```

The Ansible configuration is defined in:

```text
ansible.cfg
```

Example:

```ini
[defaults]
inventory = inventory.ini
host_key_checking = False
```

---

# 7. Update Ansible Inventory

Open the inventory file:

```bash
nano inventory.ini
```

Add the two EC2 instances:

```ini
[webservers]
server1 ansible_host=<EC2_IP_1> ansible_user=ubuntu ansible_ssh_private_key_file=/home/<USER>/.ssh/portfolio-key
server2 ansible_host=<EC2_IP_2> ansible_user=ubuntu ansible_ssh_private_key_file=/home/<USER>/.ssh/portfolio-key
```

Replace:

```text
<EC2_IP_1>
<EC2_IP_2>
<USER>
```

with the appropriate values for your environment.

---

# 8. Update the Docker Image in Ansible Playbook

Open the Ansible playbook:

```bash
nano playbook.yml
```

Update the Docker image to the image that was pushed to Docker Hub:

```yaml
name: <DOCKERHUB_USERNAME>/my-own-images:portfolio-app-v3
```

Make sure the Docker Hub repository name and image tag/version match the image created in Step 2.

The playbook handles the complete server configuration and application deployment:

```text
Install Docker
      ↓
Start Docker
      ↓
Pull Docker Image
      ↓
Remove Existing Container
      ↓
Start Application Container
```

---

# 9. Test Ansible Connectivity

Before deploying the application, verify that Ansible can connect to both EC2 instances:

```bash
ansible all -m ping
```

Both servers should return a successful response:

```text
server1 | SUCCESS
server2 | SUCCESS
```

---

# 10. Deploy the Application

Run the Ansible playbook:

```bash
ansible-playbook playbook.yml
```

The same playbook is executed against both EC2 instances.

```text
                  Ansible
                     │
           ┌─────────┴─────────┐
           ▼                   ▼
        EC2 #1              EC2 #2
           │                   │
        Docker              Docker
           │                   │
       Flask App           Flask App
```

---

# 11. Check Docker Containers

If the deployment completes successfully, verify the containers on both servers:

```bash
ansible webservers -m shell -a "docker ps"
```

To include stopped containers:

```bash
ansible webservers -m shell -a "docker ps -a"
```

---

# 12. Check Application Logs

If there is any deployment or application error, check the container logs:

```bash
ansible webservers -m shell -a "docker logs portfolio-app"
```

Check the Docker service:

```bash
ansible webservers -m shell -a "sudo systemctl status docker --no-pager"
```

Check the Docker images:

```bash
ansible webservers -m shell -a "docker images"
```

Check whether the Flask application is responding from inside the EC2 instances:

```bash
ansible webservers -m shell -a "curl -s http://localhost:5000"
```

---

# 13. Verify the Application

Once the deployment succeeds, use the public IP addresses obtained from Terraform.

Open the first EC2 instance:

```text
http://<EC2_IP_1>:5000
```

Open the second EC2 instance:

```text
http://<EC2_IP_2>:5000
```

Both instances should display the Flask application.

